WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:05.410
The thrilling first season of Shadow and Bone
is stacked with easter eggs, secret cameos,

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:09.660
and hidden clues revealing what we can expect
in future seasons of the show.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.969
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm revealing 27 crucial details

00:00:13.969 --> 00:00:17.300
you missed in Shadow and Bone and how they
affect Season 2 and beyond.

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:20.380
Spoilers for the TV show and books ahead.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.430
The Crows are arguably some of the biggest
fan favourite characters in the Grishaverse,

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:28.630
and the show made sure that Kaz and Inej deliver
one of their most famous phrases from the

00:00:28.630 --> 00:00:29.630
novels.

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:30.630
"No one's dying today.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:31.630
No mourners."

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:32.630
"No funerals."

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:36.530
In the books, the idea behind this expression
is that it's another way of saying good luck

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:41.420
before undertaking a dangerous endeavour,
somewhat similar perhaps to the phrase "Not

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:44.320
today" from another fantasy epic, Game of
Thrones.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.110
"What do we say to the god of death?"

00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:48.110
"Not today."

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:53.120
'No mourners' is also the title of the episode
finale, a shout-out that book fans will appreciate

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.449
given that all of the main characters survive
crossing the Fold in that episode, even the

00:00:57.449 --> 00:00:59.789
Darkling, aka General Kirigan.

00:00:59.789 --> 00:01:02.819
Many of the other episode titles are also
nods to the books.

00:01:02.819 --> 00:01:07.299
For example, the seventh episode is titled
"The Unsea", an alternate name used for the

00:01:07.299 --> 00:01:10.810
Shadow Fold in the novels
And it's in this episode where we go back

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:25.789
several centuries to learn about the events
that led to 

00:01:25.789 --> 00:01:32.280
the Fold's creation.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:36.619
Also near that map is the stolen DeKappel
painting that Rotty was asking Kaz about in

00:01:36.619 --> 00:01:37.619
the first episode.

00:01:37.619 --> 00:01:42.130
"The thief had to get past four roving guards,
high fences, padlocked doors, and a security

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:44.469
system designed by one of them Grisha witches."

00:01:44.469 --> 00:01:48.820
The reason for this namedrop isn't just to
show what an expert thief Kaz is,

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:52.950
it's also setting up the heist that Kaz is
recruited for in the Six of Crows book

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:58.180
where the merchant Van Eck who Kaz stole the
DeKappel painting from hires Kaz for a job

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:02.700
to kidnap a Shu scientist who developed a
Grisha drug called Jurda Parem.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:07.299
And the Jurda plant, which Jurda Parem is
made from, is also mentioned later in the

00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:11.180
show when the Conductor lists it as one of
the ingredients he needs for the trip across

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:12.180
the fold.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:15.720
"To cross, I'll need 20 pounds of alabaster
coal.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.709
A peck of Majdaloun jurda.

00:02:17.709 --> 00:02:18.870
Not the kind from Kerch.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.030
It's too weak."

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:24.209
We can see how both the DeKappel and the Jurda
mentions in this first season may be setting

00:02:24.209 --> 00:02:29.000
up the Jurda Parem plot for either season
two or a spin-off series for the Crows.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.430
Leigh Bardugo, author of the Shadow and Bone
trilogy, the Six of Crows duology, and the

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:37.849
wider Grishaverse book series, has been heavily
involved in the TV adaptation.

00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:42.360
And on top of that, the best-selling author
made a secret cameo in the third episode of

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:43.360
Season 1.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:48.629
Bardugo is dressed in a purple Durast Fabrikator
kefta, and she's the first Grisha to hug Alina

00:02:48.629 --> 00:02:53.110
after her successful display of Sun Summoning
powers at the Ravkan court.

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:57.140
And one of Bardugo's books, "The Lives of
Saints", makes a brief appearance too as the

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:00.190
book that the Apparat gives to Alina in the
palace library.

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:01.190
"A gift.

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:02.340
To mark our new friendship."

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:03.720
"The Lives of the Saints?"

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:08.000
As well as being a real-world book that Bardugo
wrote as a companion to the Grishaverse,

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:12.409
The Lives of Saints is also an in-universe
compilation of stories about Ravka's legendary

00:03:12.409 --> 00:03:16.790
saints that will come in handy for Alina in
later seasons as she learns more about Ilya

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:20.220
Morozova and his amplifiers,
and discovers that one of the illustrations

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:24.360
of Saint Ilya in the book reveals some crucial
information about him.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:29.100
The quest for Morozova's stag and its antlers
which the Darkling uses as an amplifier on

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:32.590
Alina are a big part of the final two episodes
of the first season,

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:36.640
with Kirigan binding himself to the antlers
and Alina through a Grisha ritual.

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:41.099
An intriguing detail is that the antlers being
fused to Alina's neck and her forced connection

00:03:41.099 --> 00:03:45.670
to Kirigan was foreshadowed right back in
the first episode when Alina is on the skiff

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:50.019
about to enter the Fold for the first time,
and her scarf blows off from around her neck

00:03:50.019 --> 00:03:52.900
and the wind takes it back towards General
Kirigan.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:57.180
Alina losing the scarf could also be a reference
to how in the books she uses a scarf to cover

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:58.500
up Morozova's collar.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:03.240
In the TV show, Alina won't need a scarf to
cover up the antler collar because she breaks

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:07.200
the Darkling's connection over her, and also
because after that she absorbs the antlers

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.930
into her body.

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:13.310
Talking of amplifiers, Morozova's stag also
appeared on the pages of the book that the

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:14.909
Apparat showed to Alina.

00:04:14.909 --> 00:04:19.019
The other two creatures on this page are two
more fabled beasts and living amplifiers that

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:22.590
we can expect to appear in the show's second
and subsequent seasons.

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:27.190
First is the Sea Whip, a dragon prince said
to be cursed to live as a sea serpent in waters

00:04:27.190 --> 00:04:29.350
known as the Bone Road.

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:31.400
"The bone road ebbs and the bone road flows."

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:35.180
And notice how in this scene the Apparat is
standing in front of a picture of what looks

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:39.300
like some kind of boat, pointing to the sea
journey I expect Alina will go in on in the

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:41.700
second season in search of the Sea Whip.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:46.360
The other creature is the Firebird, a mythical
beast considered crucial to the identity of

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:51.290
the Ravkan nation, and reputed to be the third
amplifier created by Morozova.

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:55.700
The hunt for the Firebird will likely be in
the third season or later depending on how

00:04:55.700 --> 00:05:00.009
the showrunner decides to continue adapting
the Grishaverse trilogy, given the first season

00:05:00.009 --> 00:05:03.440
also added in characters from the Six of Crows
duology.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:07.990
The TV show's depiction of the Fold and the
murderous Volcra beasts has been pretty impressive

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:12.900
as getting CGI creatures to look good can
be tricky especially on TV budgets.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:17.819
VFX supervisor Ted Rae was visually inspired
by gargoyles and demons for the look of the

00:05:17.819 --> 00:05:19.470
Shadow Fold monsters.

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:24.770
And there's a shout-out to that when the Darkling
stands right between two gargoyles as he calls

00:05:24.770 --> 00:05:28.280
on the forbidden merzost magic that ends up
creating the Fold and the volcra.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:29.483
If you find the geography of Shadow and Bone
a lot to take in, then it's worth taking a

00:05:29.483 --> 00:05:30.483
look at the map of the Grishaverse which you
can find on Bardugo's official website.

00:05:30.483 --> 00:05:31.483
In the show you can spot that map framed on
the wall inside Kaz's office just behind his

00:05:31.483 --> 00:05:32.483
desk where it also serves to hide his safe
of valuables.

00:05:32.483 --> 00:05:34.401
A vital member of the Crows crew will also
be Nina who meets Kaz, Inej and Jesper at

00:05:34.401 --> 00:05:35.630
the end of season 1.

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:36.630
"I have a plan.

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:37.630
We're going to need a Heartrender."

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:42.770
As a Grisha spy, Nina can speak six languages,
and there's a fun moment earlier on when as

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:46.880
she describes the Wandering Isle to Matthias
she slips into an Irish accent.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:48.090
"Oh, the Wandering Isle.

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:49.500
Well, I can fit in there, no trouble."

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:53.620
This is a reference to how the Wandering Isle
in the Grishaverse is based on Ireland, and

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:57.840
it's also a nod to the fact that the actress
who plays Nina, Danielle Galligan, is Irish

00:05:57.840 --> 00:05:58.840
herself.

00:05:58.840 --> 00:06:03.310
There's a nice moment during Nina and Matthias's
enemies-turned-lovers storyline where Nina

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:06.090
gets to enjoy some waffles with the burly
Fjerdan.

00:06:06.090 --> 00:06:08.590
Book readers will know that Nina is obsessed
with waffles.

00:06:08.590 --> 00:06:12.440
"I can't wait to introduce you to my truest
love."

00:06:12.440 --> 00:06:16.220
And her love for the battered treats would
probably even rival any romantic feelings

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:17.389
she has for Matthias.

00:06:17.389 --> 00:06:20.280
"Is this supposed to be sweet or savory?

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:21.280
Yes."

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:24.530
These next few easter eggs include spoilers
for the ending of the books, so if you want

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:28.680
to avoid these, you can skip them by going
to the timestamp on screen.

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:32.250
Although the Darkling appears to be immortal,
"He is eternal."

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:37.610
in the final episode Inej manages to throw
one of her daggers straight into his chest.

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:43.470
Kirigan however seems to call upon his merzost
powers and pulls the blade out unharmed, exclaiming

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:45.570
"It will take more than this!"

00:06:45.570 --> 00:06:50.120
So could Kirigan's words be hinting towards
the fate that awaited him in Ruin and Rising,

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:54.640
the final book in the Shadow and Bone trilogy,
where Alina kills the Darkling when she stabs

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:57.479
him with a blade made of Grisha steel?

00:06:57.479 --> 00:07:01.820
And although Inej loses that dagger during
the battle inside the Fold, later on Alina

00:07:01.820 --> 00:07:03.910
gives Inej her own blade as a replacement.

00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:06.259
"It's not much, but it'll fend off a bully
or two."

00:07:06.259 --> 00:07:07.430
"I know just what to name it."

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:11.770
In the books, Inej names her knives after
Ravkan Saints, and although she doesn't say

00:07:11.770 --> 00:07:16.610
it here, we can expect that she'll name that
knife Sankta Alina, as she has a knife called

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:18.020
this in Six of Crows.

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:22.440
I wonder whether the TV show will have Inej
kill the Darkling with the blade that Alina

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:26.169
gave her, rather than Alina doing it herself
as she does in the books?

00:07:26.169 --> 00:07:30.560
Perhaps, Inej failing to kill him the first
time around was simply the set-up for her

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:36.120
succeeding later on, and wouldn't it be poetic
if she did it with a knife called Sankta Alina?

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:40.320
Another easter egg for book readers is when
Zoya warns Alina about becoming a martyr.

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:41.940
"So remember this.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:45.310
Saints become martyrs before they get to be
heroes.

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:46.330
So...
stay alive."

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:51.050
That may be hinting at how, at the end of
Ruin and Rising, Alina fakes her own death.

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:56.199
Sankta Alina goes on to become a fabled Ravkan
martyr, while Alina and Mal each take on new

00:07:56.199 --> 00:07:59.240
names and go off to live a quiet life together.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:03.090
And if you want to dive deeper into the ending
of Shadow and Bone's first season and some

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:07.750
Season 2 theories including whether Mal has
secret Grisha powers, tap here to watch that

00:08:07.750 --> 00:08:11.020
video or follow the link in the video description.

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:14.409
As we can see, a lot of attention and detail
has been put into Shadow and Bone's first

00:08:14.409 --> 00:08:16.099
season, and the costumes are no exception.

00:08:16.099 --> 00:08:20.390
For example, the thorn patterns on the Grisha
keftas are a reference to Leigh Bardugo's

00:08:20.390 --> 00:08:24.190
The Language of Thorns book which is a collection
of Grisha folk tales.

00:08:24.190 --> 00:08:29.370
The embroidery on Alina's kefta forms a sun
symbol which is similar to the Sun Summoner

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:33.050
sunburst symbol used to represent her elsewhere
in the show.

00:08:33.050 --> 00:08:38.040
And Morozova's stag antlers form a similar
sun symbol referencing why Alina has always

00:08:38.040 --> 00:08:43.280
felt connected to the stag through her dreams
and also why it bestowed its power on her.

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:47.270
And Shadow and Bone even worked with linguist
David Peterson to create several original

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:51.500
languages on the show including developing
the Ravkan, Fjerdan and Kerch languages we

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:52.670
hear.

00:08:52.670 --> 00:08:59.100
Peterson is perhaps best known for developing
the Dothraki language used in Game of Thrones.

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:03.830
And in a humorous meta moment, the book that
David throws at Jesper when the Crows are

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:08.140
kicking him out of his carriage is a Ravkan
version of Bardugo's Shadow and Bone book

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:09.490
that was specially made for the show.

00:09:09.490 --> 00:09:11.010
"He threw a book at me."

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:15.810
I'm looking forward to Season 2 and I'm curious
to see how Kirigan will track down Alina and

00:09:15.810 --> 00:09:20.320
Mal, though with his Grisha spies everywhere,
he'll doubtless soon be on their trail.

